  <ym-title|Local stationary face estimates><label|local-stationary-face-estimates>

  <section|Status and scope><label|status-and-scope>

  Draft, awaiting independent mathematical and source review. Source pin:
  <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464> from
  <verbatim|source/upstream.json>. Owned prose:
  <verbatim|manuscript/existence/annulus-fleet-face-estimates.tex>.

  The prose proves the price-to-face calculation, the all-centered and shell
  scale substitutions, and the selected-bed row identities (F14)--(F16). It
  derives the scale-weighted input prices (F6a)--(F6d) from the selected path
  packages: tab budget (L), one-leg budget (L/T), and two-leg budget (L/T^2),
  with (L=T^2) for the interacting row and (L=\\mu^2) for the regulated
  reference. The shell difference prices follow from the cubic tab contact,
  the common linear coefficient of the interacting and free marked rows, and
  the regulator contribution. It also derives metric normalization by
  cancelling the regulator tab in the same House identity, derives the free
  symbol identity from the degree-zero exterior Cartan identity, computes the
  vacuum marked row's first radial coefficient from the three plaquette
  placements and the fixed free film, and proves the total denominator's
  zeroth inverse bound from the bed floor and cold lower bound. The halo
  prices are shell-only because the unregulated reference inverse is
  undefined at the center.

  <section|Source correspondence><label|source-correspondence>

  All paths below are in the pinned <verbatim|Lean/> source.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.Jam>, <verbatim|Fiesta>,
    <verbatim|Fiesta.pair>, <verbatim|Fiesta.plus>, <verbatim|Fiesta.inv>,
    <verbatim|Fiesta.invDiff>, <verbatim|Jam.rebounds>, <verbatim|Jam.sweet>:
    <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/Weights.lean>. These implement the
    derivative price calculus (F1)--(F3); in this application
    <verbatim|vflux> is the Euclidean operator norm, and the trace estimate
    is <verbatim|flux_trace> with the finite matrix dimension factor.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.Fleet>, <verbatim|Fleet.basic>,
    <verbatim|Fleet.bubbleHV>, <verbatim|Fleet.focus_bill>,
    <verbatim|Fleet.tadControl>, <verbatim|Fleet.bubbleControl>,
    <verbatim|Fleet.cool0>, <verbatim|Fleet.brass>,
    <verbatim|Fleet.haloControl>: <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/Propagators.lean>.
    <verbatim|Fleet.basic> merges the interacting Week at scale
    <verbatim|T^2> with the reference Week at scale <verbatim|mu^2>, using
    <verbatim|mu\<leq\>T>. <verbatim|bubbleHV> prices the full upper and
    lower four-factor expressions. <verbatim|focus_bill> takes the order-two
    price and applies <verbatim|\|tr M\|\<leq\>dim(E) vflux(M)>.
    <verbatim|tadControl> splits the upper arch into the reference term
    (price <verbatim|mu^2/T^2> at derivative scale <verbatim|T>) and the
    interacting term (unit-scale arch price plus the total inverse at its
    zeroth-order price <verbatim|T^-2>). <verbatim|bubbleControl> follows
    from <verbatim|bubbleHV> and <verbatim|focus_bill>.

    <item>For the halo difference, <verbatim|cool0> gives the
    inverse-denominator difference price <verbatim|rimEdge/T^2>;
    <verbatim|sepJ> gives the displaced-leg difference price <verbatim|T^2>,
    which is enlarged to <verbatim|rimEdge*T> because
    <verbatim|T\<leq\>rimEdge>; <verbatim|brass> combines these four receipts
    by the ordered product-difference identity. <verbatim|haloControl> takes
    its trace price. These are exactly the four summands of (F9), in the
    order of the noncommuting factors, not an estimate for an unordered
    scalar product.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.Rally.coin>, <verbatim|Rally.register>,
    <verbatim|Rally.shell>, <verbatim|Rally.bait>, <verbatim|haloRate>,
    <verbatim|poleVec>: <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/Bubbles.lean>
    and <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/Propagators.lean>.
    <verbatim|Rally.coin> is the fixed color coefficient
    <verbatim|1/2*(1/2*rod_interacting-rod_reference)>;
    <verbatim|rod_plucked> writes each rod as upper minus lower.
    <verbatim|Rally.register> combines two full-face bounds.
    <verbatim|Rally.shell> uses the halo identity and the two upper/lower
    estimates. The continuum halo-to-pole calculation is the preceding
    manuscript proposition <verbatim|prop:ex-two-wave>.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.Hstar>, <verbatim|Orion>,
    <verbatim|BundleS>, <verbatim|Looming>, <verbatim|Bundled>,
    <verbatim|Looms>, <verbatim|getReals>, <verbatim|astronomy>,
    <verbatim|Waves1>, <verbatim|Waves2>:
    <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/Bubbles.lean>. <verbatim|Looms>
    proves the all-centered ColdR conditions with scale <verbatim|flay(x)+mu>
    and cap <verbatim|1+2*pi>. For the shell, <verbatim|astronomy> restricts
    to <verbatim|mu\<leq\>flay(x)> and changes the scale to
    <verbatim|flay(x)>.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.bedLum>, <verbatim|howlin>,
    <verbatim|inflamed>, <verbatim|jarBurst>, <verbatim|barePost>,
    <verbatim|granite_tab>, <verbatim|rockEdge>, <verbatim|vectorPing>:
    <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/Leading.lean>. <verbatim|barePost>
    follows by expanding the same-bed House equality and cancelling the
    regulator tab. The proof of <verbatim|bedLum> then subtracts the leader
    identity: the metric difference is first-order at zero by
    <verbatim|Floor.starts>, the two edge symbols add two powers, and
    <verbatim|rockEdge> supplies the cubic difference between the exact
    lattice scalar and its quadratic part. For marked rows,
    <verbatim|granary> is the local film plus the fixed free film;
    <verbatim|inflamed> splits the two-leg coefficient into its local soft
    part (<verbatim|jarBurst>, order two) and the grey vacuum-minus-vector
    part (<verbatim|howlin>, order two). <verbatim|vectorPing> proves the
    vacuum first radial coefficient: <verbatim|coronet> sums the three Wilson
    plaquette placements and <verbatim|axePing> gives the fixed free-film
    coefficient; their sum is exactly the displayed vector insertion. It is
    therefore derived in the prose, not retained as an independent identity
    assumption.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.dais_floor>, <verbatim|throne_floor>,
    <verbatim|granaryTall>, <verbatim|grainTall>, <verbatim|solidT>,
    <verbatim|getReals>, <verbatim|glaze>:
    <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/Spectral/AuxReferences.lean>,
    <verbatim|OneLoop/Propagators.lean>, and <verbatim|OneLoop/Bubbles.lean>.
    Combining <verbatim|dais_floor> with <verbatim|cold_safe> gives
    <verbatim|min(epsilon,1)/2^16> times the square of the scale as a lower
    eigenvalue bound for the interacting denominator; <verbatim|throne_floor>
    gives <verbatim|2^-16> for the ghost denominator. <verbatim|solidT> turns
    these stable lower bounds into the required total inverse control.
    <verbatim|getReals> assembles both Floe/Week/Tall packages from the same
    <verbatim|Hstar> sequence, <verbatim|Orion> House/floor/fee/soft fields,
    common grey reference and centered ColdR data. The separate shell
    comparisons <verbatim|cool0>, <verbatim|sepJ>, <verbatim|brass>, and
    <verbatim|haloControl> require positive unregulated scale and are only
    applied on <verbatim|mu \<less\>= \|x\|>.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.price_step_metric>, <verbatim|bed_gain>:
    <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/Loops/Fourier.lean>. The latter adds the two
    first-order edge-symbol factors to an order-one metric difference.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.patrol_president>, <verbatim|patrol_house>,
    <verbatim|Patrol.marries>, <verbatim|sipPatrol>, <verbatim|sipLight>,
    <verbatim|ashCost1/2/3>, <verbatim|limo_cast>:
    <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/Propagators.lean> and
    <verbatim|Weights.lean>. Fee bounds and Cauchy estimates give uniform
    compact derivative caps. <verbatim|Patrol> records these caps through the
    required derivative order; <verbatim|sipPatrol> restricts them to the
    selected line (x+t e), using \PRays\Q vanishing and \Pdawn\Q's Taylor
    remainder estimate, while <verbatim|sipLight> supplies the bounded-radius
    arch price. Radial vanishing forces lower homogeneous Taylor tensors to
    vanish because each is a polynomial zero on every real direction. This
    yields the (T^3) tab remainder, (T^2) insertion remainder, and unit-scale
    arch bound used in the manuscript.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.Week>, <verbatim|Week.pays>,
    <verbatim|Week.spot>, <verbatim|Week.basic>, <verbatim|Fleet.basic>,
    <verbatim|Fleet.bubbleHV>, <verbatim|Fleet.tadControl>,
    <verbatim|Fleet.bubbleControl>, <verbatim|Tall.storms>,
    <verbatim|Tall.beats>, <verbatim|Floe.vector>, <verbatim|Floe.ghost>,
    <verbatim|sepJ>, <verbatim|bloomJ>, and <verbatim|Fleet.cool0>:
    <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/Propagators.lean>. In <verbatim|Week
    X T L>, the tab has budget (L), each spot has budget (T^{-1}L), and each
    arch has budget (T^{-2}L). <verbatim|getReals> supplies the
    interacting-plus-reference path with interacting tab budget (T^2),
    regulated reference budget (mu^2), and a <verbatim|Tall> bound at (T^2).
    Hence the total denominator path has tab budget (O(T^2)), its inverse
    paths have budget (O(T^{-2})), the local and reference arches have
    budgets (O(1)) and (O(\\mu^2/T^2)), and total legs have budget
    (O(T+\\mu^2/T)=O(T)). On the shell, the free scalar denominator has
    inverse norm (T^{-2}); the local-minus-free tab costs (T^3), the
    regulator costs (mu^2), and the inverse identity gives
    (O((T^3+\\mu^2)/T^4)=O(\\delta/T^2)). For legs, the local row minus the
    free Wilson leg has Ray order two, while the reference leg costs
    (O(\\mu^2/T)); together these give (O(\\delta T)). These are the
    quantitative derivations of the stated F6 prices, rather than additional
    price assumptions.

    <item>The regulated reference's component prices are traced through
    <verbatim|cinderWeek>, <verbatim|cinderTall>, <verbatim|chertWeek>,
    <verbatim|cannonWeek>, <verbatim|stoneWeek>, and <verbatim|glaze> in
    <verbatim|Propagators.lean>. The massive free film has tab budget (T^2)
    and scalar floor (T^2/64); the inverse-reference film's tab/spot/arch
    costs are (mu^2/T^2,mu^2/T^3,mu^2/T^4). Enlarging its tab budget to (1),
    taking the nine-factor product, weighting by (mu^2), and adding four
    constant directional sandwiches gives the actual reference path with
    costs (mu^2,mu^2/T,mu^2/T^2).

    <item>The exact inverse-film component identities and product slot
    formulas used in that calculation are <verbatim|Film.vSpot>,
    <verbatim|Film.back>, and <verbatim|Film.pay> in
    <verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/Loops/Syntax.lean>. A back spot has two
    inverse tabs and one original spot; a back arch has the two ordered
    spot-insertion terms and the original arch term, each flanked by inverse
    tabs. A product spot has two summands and a product arch four. The
    manuscript expands these formulas and the inverse-reference costs before
    iterating the nine factors.

    <item><verbatim|Jar.soft> in <verbatim|OneLoop/Lattice.lean> is the exact
    soft-3 convention: the unmarked layer has radial order three, the
    one-mark layer order two, and the two-mark layer order one.
    <verbatim|jarBurst> composes the one-mark layer with a linear
    two-momentum map; <verbatim|inflamed> adds the grey-vacuum/free
    second-order contact. This is the source of the marked-row contact, not a
    claim that every component of the action vanishes to degree three.
  </itemize-minus>

